diff --git a/docs/openmenu.md b/docs/openmenu.md
index 4400b78..623f84a 100644
--- a/docs/openmenu.md
+++ b/docs/openmenu.md
@@ -48,7 +48,7 @@ The other direction: openMenu on the Dreamcast tells the Pi what is on its SD ca
   - `LAUNCH <product>` once, when a launch is queued
   - `NEED games` when the Pi's copy of the game list does not match the hash (or there is none)
   - `NET dcnow` or `NET dcnet`: the selected network (`dcnet` only when DreamPi can use it, the same rule as `GET /tag`)
-  - `PLAYING game02 game07`: the **folders** of the card's games that someone plays online right now (up to 16, each once; the line is left out when nobody does). A folder is the `folder` field the Dreamcast uploaded; one that is not a single word (letters, digits, `_ . -`) is replaced by the product code. *This takes "folder" as openMenu's own name for the game's entry (game02); if openMenu means something else, `ident()` in `netswitch_openmenu.py` is the one place to change.*
+  - `PLAYING 2 7`: the **slots** of the card's games that someone plays online right now (up to 16, each once; the line is left out when nobody does). A slot is the game's place on the card, the number of its SD card folder, which openMenu uploads as the `slot` field (the upload's `folder` is the game's virtual folder in the Folders view, shared by several games, so it is not used). A game without a slot is named by its product code.
   - Lines an older openMenu does not know should be ignored; the new ones come after the old ones.
 - `POST /openmenu/games` with `X-Requested-With: openMenu`; body `#openmenu-games 1 <hash> <count>`, then one game per line, tab separated: product, slot, disc, region, folder, name. Kept in `openmenu_games.json` (`core.OPENMENU_GAMES`), at most 5000 games.
 
